  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nTonight into Sunday, our area will remain in between a weak system \nlifting northeast across the southeast U.S. and an approaching \ntrough axis from the west. This will lead to continued warm and dry \nconditions through the day on Sunday. We could see some mid and high\nlevel clouds associated with the southeast system affect parts of \nour area and this may help mitigate river valley fog later tonight to\nsome degree, at least across our southeast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nA weakening mid level trough axis will move east across the area \nSunday night, followed by a stronger mid level short wave that will \nmove across the Great Lakes region Monday. Shower activity will \noverspread our area from the west Sunday evening/night, but given the\nweakening trend of the trough, overall QPF looks to be fairly light.\n\nA colder airmass will begin to settle into the area later Sunday \nnight and into the day on Monday in a developing low level CAA \npattern. Highs on Monday will only be in the mid 40s to lower 50s. \nThe cold airmass will then remain in place across the region through\nthe rest of the week, with daytime highs in the 40s and overnight \nlows mostly in the mid 20s to mid 30s. \n\nThere remains a fair amount of variance in the models for mid week with\na southern stream system lifting northeast and possibly phasing with\nan approaching northern stream system. This will likely lead to a \nchance of showers Wednesday into Thursday. Ptype looks to be mainly \nrain, but thermal profiles may be just cool enough to allow for some\nwet snow to mix in at times.",
